{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/406217-help-online-gaming-best-way-to-run-a-game-online","id":406217,"title":"[Help][Online Gaming]Best Way to Run a Game Online.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-02-14T18:58:45-08:00","id":406217,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> [Help][Online Gaming]Best Way to Run a Game Online.","content":"I have story told for damn near 25 years. I also know a Storyteller should never be afraid to learn something new. <br /> <br /> I have always run my games on Table Top Setting except for my stint working in White Wolf's New Bremen, which was Chat Based. Those who know my ST name would also know my main character's name, which is my account name. <br /> <br /> So what is my issue?<br /> <br /> I was working on a Table Top Game worked out my background and the like. Even made a Webpage to promote my game and have game information. This is a Werewolf 20th Anniversary game. <br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://rage-across-the-rockies.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">https://rage-across-the-rockies.obsidianportal.com/</a><br /> <br /> I have been trying to recruit for the game here locally and no success, but I kept being asked by people many of whom are from my old gaming group to run the game online and some new faces. I now have a full pack and 1st game will be March 1st. Need to learn quickly and send the players what they need to know for the added information and success for my game. <br /> <br /> I agreed, but I never ran a game online like this. I have played some games on Roll20 and some Google + games much to no success. <br /> <br /> So I am looking for some advice and suggestions from people on what I should consider for the game from techniques to webpages to technologies I may not have considered. <br /> <br /> This is what I am looking at right now to run this and please if you see something I am missing and not don't be afraid to say something. I pride my games to last no less than a year. Most last 2-3 years. A trend I want to continue in this new style.<ul><li><b>CHAT: </b>Google + Group or Roll20 not sure.... Is there a better one?</li> <li><b>DICE ROLLER: </b>No Idea on a Good Universal Online One.</li> <li><b>MAP/PICTURE: </b>Use my website or some other place?</li> </ul>Anything I am missing? I want to get a chance to get the ball rolling here and to be honest none of the players have really online gamed either. <br /> <br /> Help would be appreciated. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4949-nene-richards","name":"Nene Richards","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4949_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"70"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-14T19:38:00-08:00","id":406275,"title":"","content":"Depending on how critical the visual side of things is, you can also run with pretty much any of the voice chat like teamspeak.<br /> <br /> Last game I ran (shadowrun) started off on Roll20 running as an app on a google hangout thing, it allowed me to show pictures to people as needed and include video if desired and all that good stuff. moved off it due to one of the players having bandwidth issues.<br /> <br /> Dice rollers I honestly don't bother with (there's a basic one built into roll 20 if you want. <br /> <br /> Visuals are a big help, maps and general layouts otherwise things get painful.<br /> <br /> As for the hardware requirements, decent bandwidth all around, make sure everyone has headphones and microphones... you want to avoid using the ones built into the computer, because then you have to deal with configuration and trying to compensate for feedback and unless all the players are technical that's not going to work out well. best is to just make sure everyone is using headphones and use push-to-talk so it doesn't continually pick up background noises.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-14T21:15:57-08:00","id":406330,"title":"","content":"I pretty much play online only. Roll20 for visuals (drop in your own) and dice rolling, skype for voice chat (we don't do cam). The roller in roll20 handles everything we throw at it, with macros being a huge help. You can set up multiple maps, and tab between them. You can draw and ping the map, measure stuff, all sorts of things that are helpful.<br /> <br /> So our set up for the five different systems is the same: roll20, skype, and headsets. For me and my husband echoes and feedback can be a pain when we play in the same room, or if someone tries downloading/uploading there is interference.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4822-loup","name":"Loup","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4822_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}